r586 | donghao | 2020-06-13 01:17:22 -0600 (Sat, 13 Jun 2020) | 47 lines

1. Fixed a queer bug that could cause MPI_rank errors in some cases. 
   Change the STATUS array size in Declaration_MPI module to be 
   "MPI_STATUS_SIZE". This (array size) was set to 5, while the value 
   seems to be 6 in modern MPI libs (in MPI/Declaration_MPI.f90). This 
   may cause some out-of-boundary problems. 
   This, I believe, is the origin of some strange errors in 
   stable-
   Note this may not work in extremely old MPI libs (i.e. MPI 1.0 era). 

2. Fixed another bug in the pack_userdef_control and unpack_userdef_control
   in module Sub_MPI.
   long story short, the sequences of the ctrl parameters are messed up
   between the two. like pack: 1,2,3 -->unpack: 1,2,4
   the pack subroutine exploits the fact that all the ctrl parameters are 
   stored one after another. So instead of doing the pack 20 times with 
   a 80-character string each time, the subroutine pack a 20*80 memory block 
   all at once. 
   However, the actual ctrl parameters are 21 instead of 20 - the pack 
   subroutine did not pack rFile_Prior, while the unpack subroutine 
   failed to unpack wFile_Sens...
   I just re-arranged the ctrl parameters (in UserCtrl.f90) and the unpack
   subroutine (in 3D_MT/Sub_MPI.f90) so that the pack and unpack 
   sequences match. 
   Not sure who wrote the initial version of UserCtrl.f90 - he/she should 
   look into my changes and make sure I did not touch something wrong. 

3. Fixed a memory leak in ForwardSolver (BC not deallocated in fwdSetup).
   This apparently affects all versions (including MF, SP, SP2 and SPETSc2). 
   This memleak has been there since the last year, I believe. 
   The leak will lead to a significant increase of memory usage, when the 
   program runs for some time (say, a few days), hence I would recommend
   updating to this version if you are running large inversions. 
   
   However, some compilers (Intel, for example), seems not be affected by the
   memleak as they have (sort of) better memory management.  
   I have tested the code (SP and SP2) with Valgrind and gfortran. Note there 
   are still some memleaks here and there - but most of them just have a 
   magnitude of tens of KBs - probably won't cause problems (a few megabytes)
   for modern systems. 

4. Removed a couple of intermediate arrays in SP2 and SPETSc2 (in updateCond, 
   updateFreqCond). Won't make much of real-world difference. But hey, we saved
   some memory (of size Nnode and Nedge)! 

Note I only tested the SP/SP2 versions with GFortran - not sure if SPETSc2 will 
work well with this update (it should, I believe). 

