r583 | donghao | 2020-02-25 16:49:34 -0700 (Tue, 25 Feb 2020) | 82 lines



A major Update to backport the two-layer parallelization version from ModEMdevel to stable- Now it is possible to try out the finer-grain parallelization with   ModEM and PETSc (i.e. use more CPUs than the number of Transmitters). As I reserved the necessary interfaces, it is also possible now to build your own finer-grain parallel forward scheme upon the two-layer structure (and avoid the PETSc mess). See MPI/Main_MPI.f90 for details.

Well, to use the finer-grain parallel scheme in its current form, One needs to compile ModEM with proper configured PETSc lib. Please refer to http://mcs.anl. gov/petsc/ for the (exhausting) procedures for the setup of PETSc libs.

See CONFIG/Configure.SPETSc2.MAC.GFortran for a sample configuration file for the Makefile - Note that one needs to edit the '-pdir' parameter for the location of PETSc lib, in order to tell the makefile where to find the extra include and link files.

After the building, Simply assign more CPUs than nTx (nPer*nPol+1 in MT) to try the finer-grain parallel feature. One of the potential loopholes of the new version is still the dynamic load-balancing system. Although I have been using it for a while (seems robust to me so far), it might still produce some queer results in some situations I overlooked. As far as I tried, it is good enough for my “everyday work”, although the efficiency (for both time and memory) can certainly be improved.

Note that for the finer grain parallelization to work effectively, the problem to be solved should be large enough (in the sense of the degree of freedom). Testing the PETSc version with a toy model would most certainly lead to a slow-down as the benefit of more CPUs is easily overshadowed by the extra overhead introduced.

Another thing worth noting is that now ALL other versions (MF/SP/SP2) share the same two-layered parallel structure. But only the SPETSc2 version is able to effectively utilize it (non-PETSc programs will just behave like before).    So it IS also possible to assign more CPUs than "the necessary number" in those versions, albeit the extra CPUs won't do anything but wait while others do the job.

Let me know if you encounter any problems with this version!
Hao

Detailed change logs are as follows:

MPI/Main_MPI.f90
    updated to implement the new two-layered parallel structure 
