r576 | donghao | 2019-11-12 02:11:19 -0700 (Tue, 12 Nov 2019) | 45 lines

An initial implementation for the inversion in normal or 'smooth' model space. The major motivation is to try different regularization methods in ModEM. see below email message for details.  

Files touched:
f90/UserCtrl.f90
f90/3D_MT/Main.f90
f90/3D_MT/Sub_MPI.f90
f90/INV/INVcore.f90

Files added:
f90/3D_MT/modelParam/modelCov/CovarianceOperator.inc
f90/3D_MT/modelParam/modelCov/CovarianceOperator.hd
f90/INV/LBFGS2.f90 

NOTE: to make the whole thing compile, one needs to change LBFGS.f90 to LBFGS2.f90 in Makefiles generated by Anna's config scripts
===============================================================================

Dear All, 

I am forking a new temporary branch from stable- (currently uninspiringly named "stable-ortho”). 

The basic idea is quite simple: retreat to the normal or “smooth” model space (m) from ModEM's previous rough/transformed model space (mHat) and test different model covariance operators 

Apparently other covariance operators (like L2) didn’t really go very well in transformed model space. As Gary mentioned, we probably need some cool preconditioner to make the inversion scheme stable in modified model space. Yes, there were no preconditioner for the inversion problem in ModEM as the transformed model space with 1D AR roughen-er seemed quite well conditioned. However, the laplacian roughening operator makes the transformed model space quite ill-conditioned (very rough). So far, the most obvious methods I tried (like a diagonal preconditioner or BFGS-style Hk preconditioner) didn’t really work well. (please let me know if you have good ideas about constructing a simple preconditioner in mHat space)

Ultimately, it would be nice if we can find a way to do it in transformed model space. But for now I decided to make the (trivial) move to go back to the smooth model space (like what others are doing). So far I have tried to implement 3 covTypes in normal model space. The following should work in both SP and MF forward frameworks. And you are welcomed to try them out, should you have the time. 

1) covType = 1: 1D AR - *should* act similar as in the transformed model space (default ModEM) 
2) covType = 2: L2 (Cm^0.5 = Laplacian) - a lot smoother than the AR, but can fit the data (almost) as well.  
3) covType = 3: L1 (Cm = Laplacian) -  rougher than L2 - not sure how to implement in transformed model space as one cannot easily construct a Cm^0.5. 

There is no “best” covariance operator, of course. Generally, L1 and L2 give you more large-scale structures, while AR produces more small scale blobs and splinters. The covType parameter is currently hard coded in Userctrl.f90 (I feel no point to add it in a ctrl file yet). Currently cov files are also supported for L1 and L2 operators to turn off the smoothing between certain model subdomains. I have not tested it, though. Other details can be found in 3D_MT/modelParam/modelCov/CovarianceOperator*

As I am eliminating the model perturbation mHat in the search procedure, the objective function, line search and gradient calculation subroutines should *all* be modified - for now only the LBFGS method is supported. But other methods should be easily changed to work in normal model space as well. See INV/INVcore.f90 and INV/LBFGS2.f90 for details. There are a number of other minor modifications to accommodate the above mentioned changes (like some minor tricks to pack and send the covType to parallel workers in 3D_MT/Sub_MPI.f90). 

OK, I know this will make the ModEM branches even more complicated. But it provides a testbed for new model regularization methods. Other regularizations can be easily added in CovarianceOperator include files. Now it is also trivial to get rid of the prior model in the penalty function as we are in the normal model space (the prior is still here by default currently). And it is probably a better idea to move my personal experiments in SP2 to an updated and (hopefully) more stable branch, in case any of you (or your students) want to try it. 

Regards, 
Hao


Anna - 

By mistake, I forked the whole directory of stable- (instead of just the f90 folder) to stable-ortho - sorry for that! 
You might want to throw away the other parts (example/docs, etc) to save some space on the server. 

