r535 | anya | 2018-10-17 15:17:38 -0600 (Wed, 17 Oct 2018) | 107 lines

Major release compared to old stable; getting ready to roll out the new stable version. Improvements include:

1. Air layers can now be set in the forward solver control file. Three options are: mirror, fixed height, read from file. For backwards compatibility, the default is 'mirror 10 3. 30.' Recommended option is 'fixed height 12 1000.' This was at one point tested against Matlab, but additional tests wouldn't hurt. Air layers only implemented for 3D.

2. LOG10 resistivity model file option. To exercise, create a WS or Mackie format model file with "LOG10" resistivity instead of "LOGE" or "LINEAR". Internally in the code, we are keeping LOGE for all computations; but we convert LOG10 or LINEAR to LOGE, make note of the user preference, then convert back for output.

3. Reading and writing of sparse ascii BC and/or SOURCE files (can easily switch to binary or NetCDF later). Fully developed and tested. In this release, we are not yet using these files for forward modeling: this is the next step. However, this file is now an optional input to forward modeling -F code option. Once we add this option, one could use the new [EXTRACT_BC] to quickly get started using the new sparse vector file format. The file has sparse vectors for each period & mode, and can include BC, SOURCE or both.

4. Some preparation for the new option to "dry run" the forward solver [EXTRACT_BC] - complete the set up, including boundary conditions, - then output the boundary conditions to file. The option not included yet: it involves major changes to ForwardSolver which require extra debugging and are saved for a separate release.

5. Options to generate random perturbation files for model [-A m], data [-A d], RHS [-A b], E-soln [-A e].

6. Fully developed and debugged symmetry tests, gradient test, and the full Jacobian test. The code now passes all tests; all symmetry tests (including S) are passed with arbitrary random perturbations as input. The symmetry test [TEST_ADJ] has many sub-options, see below for details.

 [TEST_GRAD]
  -g  rFile_Model rFile_Data rFile_dModel [rFile_fwdCtrl rFile_EMrhs]
   Runs the ultimate test of the gradient computation based on
   Taylor series approximation.
 [TEST_ADJ]
  -A  J rFile_Model rFile_dModel rFile_Data [wFile_Model wFile_Data]
   Tests the equality d^T J m = m^T J^T d for any model and data.
   Optionally, outputs J m and J^T d.
 [TEST_SENS]
  -S  rFile_Model rFile_dModel rFile_Data wFile_Data [rFile_fwdCtrl]
   Multiplies by the full Jacobian, row by row, to get d = J m.
   Compare to the output of [MULT_BY_J] to test [COMPUTE_J]

7. Cleaned up the handling of non-zero sources in EMsolve3D.f90 - checked the physics and maths, added comments to clarify things.

8. The data format now includes a line in the header to specify the transmitter type (one of: MT, DC, CSEM, TIDE, GLOBAL). This change is backwards compatible: both "new format" and "old format" are supported automatically in the code. Will be necessary for clean joint inversion code, once we get to it. Backward compatibility fully tested before commit. Will output in the same format as the input data file. Can be safely propagated to stable.

9. After comparing with stable version for efficiency, decided to skip the changes that employ precomputed grid elements V_E, l_E, S_E, V_F, l_F, S_F, V_N, V_C for all grid operators for now (sg_diff_oper.f90 and modelOperator3d.f90). The changes in their current state result in 20% increase in run time; the code also requires some cleaning. For now, I have only included setting up the grid operators, and a few little fixes in modelOperator3d.f90. Current version has not measurably suffered in efficiency or storage requirements. In the future, will optimize the cleaner code the uses the grid elements and make it part of the trunk, and possibly stable - but for now that is unchanged.

10. Cosmetic: replaced output format es12.6 with es13.6 throughout the code, to eliminate the flood of warnings in Intel Fortran compiler, except in inversion routines. If I made that change in NLCG, the log format would now be different, as follows...
Old format:    START: f=4.469161E+02 m2=0.000000E+00 rms=  21.140389 lambda=1.000000E+01 alpha=2.000000E+01
New format:    START: f= 4.469161E+02 m2= 0.000000E+00 rms=  21.140389 lambda= 1.000000E+01 alpha= 2.000000E+01
I decided that it would only confuse things to bundle this cosmetic change with the other stuff, so left the log files alone.

*** Both 3D_MT and 2D_MT have been thoroughly tested for MT in serial with ifort and gfortran. They pass all tests. ***

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Untested, upcoming, and known bugs:

One known bug is in Stest for 2D_MT: sparse2full_rhsVector doesn't work yet, so Stest cannot be run. Not a big deal and can be easily fixed when and if it is ever needed.
Another known bug in TEST_SENS test for 2D_MT: calcJ outputs NaN. This could mean problems computing J, or something to do with "allocated" attributes for model params.

Boundary conditions logic to be added in next release: defaults to internal computations (2D). If a nested file is specified, uses that. If an EM RHS file is provided, that supercedes both internal computations, and nested. The use of externally supplied boundary conditions not fully tested yet (but the general user won't be affected because it's a new capability).

Before we can use the new BC input functionality, CHECK POLARIZATION INDEX IN RHS IO TO MAKE SURE THAT MODES ARE NOT GETTING MIXED UP. At present, everything runs but the impedances are different if you "compute" the BC, then read it in.

NEED TESTING: NestedEM, especially with the new air layers. Further MPI inversion testing wouldn't hurt before this is sent to stable.

Relative to stable, for some reason the value of LARGE_REAL that defines the error bars for modeled data has changed from 2.0e15 to 1.0e13; will let it be since I cannot remember the reason for this. Otherwise, impedances are exactly the same unless the air layers are changed. With modified air layers, values are noticeably different and probably more accurate... because now the diagonals are exactly zero over a 1D area. Tippers are much closer to the original values than impedances with modified air layers.

We should also clean up and streamline the EXAMPLES directory.

Also, should update README.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

