> ~/Developer/ModEM/f90/Mod3DJT -g

 Usage: -g  rFile_Model rFile_Data rFile_dModel [rFile_fwdCtrl rFile_EMrhs]
 
   The ultimate test of the gradient computations. Based on the Taylor
   series approximation:
   Compute f(m0+dm) - f(m0)
   Compute df/dm|_m0 x dm as a dot product
   Compare the two resultant scalars

Example with BLOCK2 model:

~/Developer/ModEM/f90/Mod3DJT -A m mTrue.cpr delta.cpr 0.05
~/Developer/ModEM/f90/Mod3DJT -g pr.ws de.dat delta.cpr

Total derivative test based on Taylor series [f(m0+dm) ~ f(m0) + df/dm|_{m0} x dm]
  f(m0+dm) - f(m0):   -2.682541    
  df/dm|_{m0} x dm:   -2.391304    
 Exiting...
  elapsed time (mins)   0.1088684 

... modified stable (all but the grid elements) produces

Total derivative test based on Taylor series [f(m0+dm) ~ f(m0) + df/dm|_{m0} x dm]
  f(m0+dm) - f(m0):   -2.682541    
  df/dm|_{m0} x dm:   -2.391304    
 Exiting...
  elapsed time (mins)   8.4309898E-02

The difference is about 10% and stays that way when dm is gradually made smaller
(one would think it should converge to zero). So there's probably a bug somewhere in
the code. If so, it is not obvious at all. It is not critical though, things still work.

Make sure to run this test with a "real" data file that has error bars. Otherwise,
the gradient at dm=0 will obviously be tiny. The functionals are not tiny because 
of the lambda * dm^2 term. 

Note that in the limit rms->0 such as when the artifically large errors
are used, Taylor series breaks for the model norm - can't ignore the
second derivative. Then analytically, this approximation yields
f(dm) - f(0) = df/dm|_0 x dm = 0, or
f(-dm) - f(0) = df/dm|_{-dm} x {-dm} = 2 \lambda dm^2 = 2 * f(-dm)
In both of these cases, f(0) = 0 and f(dm) = f(-dm) = \lambda * dm^2.

