r392 | anya | 2012-06-27 12:22:27 -0600 (Wed, 27 Jun 2012) | 13 lines

Fixed a tiny output bug for resistivity on the grid. 
Added a new parametrization coined "exp" but in fact simply a rescaled log parameterization 
rho = rho0*exp(-rho0*m). This translates to sigma = sigma0*exp(m/sigma0), the idea being
that the derivative d\sigma/dm is independent of the background in the limit m->0.
So that around the background the derivative is asymptotically linear, and doesn't
have a preference in terms of inserting the anomalies in the more conductive (i.e. deeper!)
layers. Jin's idea is that this will make it fit the data with more near-surface structures.
Good idea, but the tests so far show that yes, it inserts near-surface structures primarily,
but that these don't fit the data nearly as well as the usual, unrescaled, version.
It would be identical to the usual log, rescaled by the prior, but it isn't due to the
thinsheet; of course, thinsheet doesn't have to be parametrized the same way.


