r379 | anya | 2012-03-15 15:31:42 -0600 (Thu, 15 Mar 2012) | 26 lines

EARTH update.
Came back after maternity leave and verified that the code is, indeed, correct.
The RMS misfit computation in the forward code is correct, too. However, the
T option (which computes the so-called "generalized magnetic field TFs", i.e.
scaled magnetic fields) outputs the magnetic fields, rescaled using the coefficient
obtained from the input data. Therefore, provides a different output than the H
option, and, more importantly, provides a data-dependent output! This needs to be
fixed. The output file should always contain the computed magnetic fields,
and nothing else.
Note, however, that the behaviour of the inversion with the T option will be very
different from that with the H option: the RMS's will be different, even when
the correct source scaling is used.
It's a little tricky; let me explain.
Magnetic fields depend on the source scaling. Scaled fields (or generalized magnetic
field TFs, T) - don't. So the RMS should of course be taken on T's!
However, the "Dst-like scaling coefficient", c, depends not only on the source
scaling and configuration (mildly on the latter, if mid-latitude observatories
are used), but also, in a non-linear way, on the model.
The ratio T will converge to the true value as the model converges to the true model;
however, the RMS will behave differently than if the fields were used, directly.
On input, we divide the data (and errors) by a data-dependent factor, c_{data}.
On output, however, we compute the fields based on the background model, and divide
them by a model (and source) dependent factor obtained from the computed fields.
So the RMS will only be identical to the H-case when the model *is* the truth.
Try it on synthetic data!

