This morning, I tried running the secondary field formulation (SFF) with a checkerboard model, and ascertained that the SFF is definitely working 
both with FD and Jin's 1D primary fields... Then I found (and fixed) one remaining bug that had something to do specifically with the way the 
thinsheet is defined in my code.

Now the good news is that both Jin's and my codes are both working and are very accurate (at least at the Earth's surface). I just compared 
Kuvshinov's C responses at 2.133 days with those computed on a 3x3 degree grid with SFF (using Jin's model as the primary), and also directly 
with the global code (no SFF).

For 1D, the C responses are

Kuvshinov: 7.6107e+002 +1.5587e+002i
SFF (Jin): 7.6192e+002 -1.5532e+002i
Global FD: 8.1153e+002 -1.5548e+002i away from the poles,
and up to 8.1465e+002 -1.5508e+002i near the poles.
... so, quite good, actually, especially with the SFF. With the thinsheet, the plots also match the Kuvshinov's responses very closely. I haven't 
compared the numbers yet.

The bad news is that in the process of rewriting the code completely I seem to have accidentally fixed the problem at the poles. It certainly 
doesn't seem to be very dramatic anymore, even without the SFF. Certainly, the SFF provides an improvement, but I don't know how major it is! I 
am currently running the code (with and without the SFF) at 1x1 degrees to check it out, and also to compare more directly with Kuvshinov's 
results. They may not complete in time, though!

So, we now finally have a working 3D global code. Having said that, there are still some caveats. First, even though Jmult / JmultT pass the 
symmetry test, the gradient test (based on the Taylor's series approximation) doesn't look too good, especially with the SFF; and my initial 
inversions all started producing unreasonable gradients at some point, and diverged. So looks like there is still some minor bug in the 
sensitivities floating around... shouldn't be a big problem, though, since I do have the old (working) inversion to compare with.
And finally, whenever I try to use the SFF (reading the primary model from file) on grids of 2x2 degrees or finer, Intel compiler gives me a 
segmentation fault even before entering the forward solver. PG compiler works, though! Something else to debug after Egypt. 

