r134 | anya | 2009-06-01 17:18:52 -0600 (Mon, 01 Jun 2009) | 29 lines

Many updates resulting from a painful week of debugging the memory leaks.
Found two serious memory leaks in 3D MT & Global:
1) dotProd routines for cvector and rvector didn't deallocate the temporary
vector they created (this vector is in fact needed for the global dot product);
2) sparse vectors were deallocating more memory then necessary, by using a long
deallocate(x,y,z...) statement that included one of the variables twice.

These were difficult to find, but otherwise obvious (here, MemoryScape helped!)
A bunch of memory leaks are still there. These are much more mysterious.

It looks like a memory leak occurs every time when
1) I use an intent(out) specification for a vector, then allocate that vector
inside a subroutine, or deallocate and then allocate;
2) I allocate a vector which is a function output.

I have already encountered problems with functions, when a call like m = c*m
and an attempt to (de)allocate the output inside the function leads to tricky
mysterious errors. Now it looks like functions create memory problems, even
if you use them correctly! The problem is, Fortran doesn't know anything about
the function output (or an intent(out) variable, it seems) inside the function
(or, respectively, subroutine). Strangely, any attempt to memory manage such 
a variable leads to memory issues.

For now, I've given up: zero is back to being a subroutine; deleted all operators 
that were overloaded with functions; making all derived type variables with
pointer (vector) contents intent(inout). This should solve the problems.
However, all this seems to disable the nicer features of Fortran, making me
love it more than ever...

