r114 | anya | 2009-03-30 16:59:43 -0600 (Mon, 30 Mar 2009) | 66 lines

Major changes to the organization of the code. Both 2D and 3D work. 

Implemented changes in DataSpace.

1) Added an extra layer in the data vector; now, dataVecMTX_t contains one component
   of type dataVecTX_t for each transmitter, and dataVecTX_t contains one component
   of type dataVec_t for each data type. So that, dataVec_t corresponds to exactly
   one data type and exactly one transmitter, and multiple data types can now be easily
   stored for a single transmitter. Should also be easier to parallelize over transmitters.
   Obviously, updated the basic operators in DataSpace to reflect this change.
   Note that now the nTx in the EMsolnMTX_t finally means the same thing as the nTx
   in dataVecMTX_t, i.e., the number of transmitters (NOT the number of data vectors).
   
2) Deleted the component nData from dataVecMTX_t. It was only duplicating existing
   information, and it wasn't very transparent. Instead, in the few occasions when it
   is needed (e.g., to allocate the sensitivity matrix) it is computed with a function.
   
3) Added the component isComplex to dataVec_t. I think that the sole purpose of 
   the dataType should be to identify the mapping from the EM solution to the data vector.
   The isComplex attribute is just as much an attribute of the data vector itself, and is
   completely general (i.e. makes sense for any possible instance of a data vector). so I
   think storing this information in the dataType instead is rather redundant. When
   isComplex is an attribute of the data vector, and as soon as we sort out the calcQ business, 
   MeasComb and SensMatrix should not need to use the type dictionary to a greater extend 
   than the transmitter or receiver dictionaries.
   Besides, this approach also allows us to do the dot products correctly in the DataSpace.
   
4) Updated SensMatrix to loop over transmitters, then over data types, where necessary.

5) Deleted Jmult_MTX and JmultT_MTX, which previously multiplied each section of J
   corresponding to one transmitter by a separate model parameter (similarly, the
   transpose computed a bunch of model parameters, one for each transmitter).
   These routines were not used by anything. We can always implement them if they are
   ever needed.
   
There are two potential causes for error:
a) accessing error in dataVec before checking the errorBar == .true.; can be avoided by
   allocating space for error even when the error bars are not defined.
b) setting the indices e.g. tx in dataVecTX before the data vector is created (the create
   subroutines initialize all indices to zero to enforce careful treatment of these indices).
Both points can be made more user-friendly (but that would allow sloppier coding!)


Changes to come involve the dictionaries and ModelSpace.

1) A cleaner way to deal with the data I/O is to place it in a module which has access
   to all dictionaries. Currently, the receiver dictionary rxDict is private to DataFunc,
   and the transmitter dictionary txDict is private to EMsolver. Instead, each dictionary
   will be placed in a separate module, and each module will only be used by the modules
   that need it. These should all be the same, except for the additional module which deals
   with the I/O and gathers all of the information together. [In the future, we might want
   to replace the integers in the EMsolver etc routines with variables of transmitter_t,
   receiver_t and dataType_t. That would make the code less error-prone, since the compiler
   would then check for any calling mistakes; and more flexible, since all the dictionary
   business would be completely transparent to the user. However, it might have undesirable
   implications in the hypothetical case of multiple dictionaries. Needs to be thought through.]
   
2) There will be an upper-level module ModelSpace that binds together the model parameter
   and the computational grid in a public data type modelVec_t. The contents of that data
   type will be private, but the grid would be accessible through a subroutine (in F2003,
   could make the model parameter private, the grid public). Then, there is no longer any
   need to hide the modelParam_t attributes and to stick everything to do with the model
   parameters in a single module. As long as the modelParam_t is never used outside of the
   modelParam set of modules, the modularity would be preserved. Instead, modelVec_t should
   be used in all of the upper-level code. The module ModelSpace shall contain the general
   purpose wrappers for all of the necessary model parameter subroutines.
