Reverted Naser Meqbel SEQUENCE statements commit (sorry Naser!)

These statements were causing warnings with the Intel compiler, like this:

fortcom: Warning: SENS/DataSpace.f90, line 62: The structure length is not a multiple of its 
largest element; could create misalignments for arrays of this type.   [DVECMTX]
  type :: dvecMTX
----------^
ifort -c -O3 3D_MT/Grid3D.f90
fortcom: Warning: 3D_MT/Grid3D.f90, line 34: The structure contains one or more misaligned 
fields. [GRID3D_T]
  type :: grid3d_t
----------^ 

Here is what I read about the SEQUENCES statement in the Fortran 
reference: "When a Fortran compiler allocates memory for a variable of a derived data type, the 
compiler is *not* required to allocate the elements in successive memory locations. This 
freedom was deliberately built into Fortran 95 and Fortran 2003 standards to allow compilers on 
massively parallel computers to optimize memory allocations for the fastest possible 
performance. However, there are times when a strict order of memory allocations is important. 
For example, if you want to pass a variable of a derived data type to a procedure written in 
another language..." 

I believe we should think and discuss this first. For now, I did
svn merge -r 91:90 http://mt.coas.oregonstate.edu/svn/ModEM/trunk/f90

I have left the isnan function in utilities.f90, after correcting the bug that caused compiler 
errors.

