Here is the main issue: all Gary's functions in Matlab operate with Matlab variables
running the Fortran program through writing and reading intermediate files, the names
of which are all hard coded in Matlab. This is nice since it makes the Matlab code
look simple and readable, but the user has no control of anything going on in the file
system. I suggest transitioning to running a Matlab version of Test2D, that calls
the Fortran program with command line arguments. The following modifications are intended
to assist in this transition:
1) Moved ascii I/O routines to ioAscii and left them alone for now;
2) Created Test2D.m that runs the Fortran program Test2D with command line arguments
   (currently, only the name of the grid file is hardcoded to Test1.grd, the rest
    could be passed as command line arguments);
3) Created two functions out of mkCond.m and named them mkCond2D.m and setCond2D.m;
4) Created a test driver program sensTest2D.m that could be run as a replacement
   to condDataMapsTest.m, that automatically creates grid and model files and the
   scratch subdirectory. Uses a symbolic link CondDataMaps that points to Test2D program.
I will next replace the calls to the Fortran program in sensTest2D.m with Test2D calls
and transparent command line arguments, using file names instead of Matlab structures;
then reading the files into Matlab structures and plotting them.
